  { q: 'What data does process mining need?', a: 'At minimum, an event log in which every row records a case identifier, an activity name and a timestamp. Additional attributes, such as the team, channel, product or value of the case, make it possible to compare variants and segments.' },
  { q: 'Is process mining the same as business process monitoring?', a: 'No. Process mining reconstructs and analyses how a process has run, usually over a historical window. <a href="/resources/business-process-monitoring/">Business process monitoring</a> watches stages, queues and commitments continuously as work flows. Many teams use mining to understand a process and monitoring to keep it healthy.' },
  { q: 'Does process mining show why a process is slow?', a: 'It shows where time is spent, which paths are common and where rework loops occur. Explaining <em>why</em> usually needs context the event log does not hold, such as a policy change, a staffing change or an upstream system issue, and should distinguish correlation from verified cause.' },
  { q: 'Can process mining be used to monitor individual employees?', a: 'Event logs often contain user identifiers, so it technically can. Panoptes is designed to observe processes and outcomes, not individuals, and recommends excluding or aggregating staff identifiers unless they are needed to route ownership.' },
];
