  <h2>How does process mining work?</h2>
  <p>Process mining starts from an <strong>event log</strong>. Every time a system records that something happened to a case (an invoice was created, approved, sent or paid), that record becomes a row with three essential fields:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Case identifier</strong>: which order, claim, ticket or application the event belongs to.</li>
    <li><strong>Activity</strong>: what happened, such as "documents received" or "payment captured".</li>
    <li><strong>Timestamp</strong>: when it happened.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Sorting the events for each case by time gives a trace: the actual path that case took. Aggregating thousands of traces produces a map of the process as it really runs, including the paths nobody drew on the official diagram. The time between consecutive events shows where cases spend their time, and repeated activities show where work loops back.</p>
  <p>The hard part is rarely the algorithm. It is building a clean log: deciding what counts as a case, mapping system-specific status codes to meaningful activities, and joining events from several systems that use different identifiers for the same customer or order.</p>

  <h2>The three main types of process mining</h2>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>Question it answers</th><th>Typical output</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Discovery</td><td>What does the process actually look like?</td><td>A process map with frequencies, durations and variants</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Conformance checking</td><td>Where does reality deviate from the intended process?</td><td>Cases that skipped approvals, ran steps out of order or repeated work</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Enhancement</td><td>How could the model be enriched or improved?</td><td>Maps annotated with waiting time, rework rates or cost by path</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2>What is process mining used for?</h2>
  <p>It is most useful where a process is high-volume, crosses several teams or systems, and is believed to work differently from how it is documented. Common examples:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Order to cash</strong>: finding the paths that delay invoicing or collection. See <a href="/resources/knowledge/what-is-order-to-cash/">what is order to cash</a>.</li>
    <li><strong>Purchase to pay</strong>: spotting invoices paid without a matching order, or approvals that are routinely bypassed.</li>
    <li><strong>Customer onboarding</strong>: seeing how many applications loop back for missing documents, and how long they wait each time.</li>
    <li><strong>Claims and case handling</strong>: identifying the variants that take far longer than the standard path.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Typical outcomes are a redesigned hand-off, a removed approval step, a targeted automation or a corrected system configuration, each of which should then be measured to confirm the change worked.</p>

  <h2>What are the limitations of process mining?</h2>
  <p>Process mining is powerful, but its boundaries are worth knowing before a project starts:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>It only sees what is logged.</strong> Work done in email, spreadsheets or conversations leaves no events, so those steps appear as unexplained waiting.</li>
    <li><strong>It is usually retrospective.</strong> Many analyses are run on an extract covering past months. That is ideal for redesign, less so for noticing that today's queue is building.</li>
    <li><strong>One process at a time.</strong> A log is built around one case notion. Effects that cross processes, such as a fulfilment delay that later shows up as a collections problem, need the processes to be connected.</li>
    <li><strong>Where, not why.</strong> A map shows where time goes. The reason, perhaps a policy change, a supplier delay or a new release, usually lives outside the log.</li>
  </ul>
  <div class="callout">
    <strong>Mapping is the start, not the finish</strong>
    The value of process mining comes from the decisions it informs and from checking afterwards whether those decisions changed the process. A map that is admired once and not revisited rarely changes outcomes.
  </div>

  <h2>Process mining vs business process monitoring</h2>
  <p>The two are complementary. Process mining is primarily an <em>analysis</em> discipline: reconstruct, compare and redesign. <a href="/resources/business-process-monitoring/">Business process monitoring</a> is an <em>operating</em> discipline: watch arrivals, waiting, work in progress, rework and breach risk continuously, and tell the owner when a stage drifts. Mining is a good way to learn the shape of a process; monitoring keeps an eye on it once you know what normal looks like.</p>

  <h2>How it relates to business observability</h2>
  <p>Business observability takes the same raw material, timestamped business events, and connects it across processes, customers, money and commitments in one continuously updated model. Rather than mining one process from a historical extract, the aim is to see how a change in one flow affects the others while it is happening.</p>
  <p>Panoptes is designed to model processes as stages with entry and exit events, measure flow at each stage and link those stages to the customers, payments and commitments they affect. Its <a href="/platform/process-intelligence/">Process Intelligence</a> capability is designed to show throughput, waiting time and constraints, and <a href="/platform/root-cause-analysis/">Root Cause Analysis</a> is designed to trace a change back to contributing causes while stating clearly what is correlation and what is confirmed.</p>
  <p>If you already use process mining, its event definitions and process maps are a useful head start for building an observable model of the same process.</p>
</KnowledgeArticle>
